response:
Denote by $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$ the intersection points. Then $(b=d)$ and since $f(x)=y$,
\begin{align*}
f(c)=f(a)
\end{align*}
Next we subtract equalities
\begin{align*}
c^2+4c+6=a^2+4a+6\text{ and } \frac{1}{2}c^2+c+6=\frac{1}{2}a^2+a+6
\end{align*}
which follow from the definition of $f(x)=y$. Eventually we have
\begin{align*}\begin{cases}
c^2-a^2+4c-4a=0\\\frac{1}{2}c^2-c-\frac{1}{2}a^2+a+6=0\end{cases}\Rightarrow \begin{cases}
2c^2+8c-8a=0\\\frac{1}{2}c^2-c-\frac{1}{2}a^2+a=0\end{cases}\Rightarrow \\c=2a
\end{align*}
Substitution leads to a quadratic equation. The solution is
\begin{align*}a=-4&\text{ or }a=-2\\ \Rightarrow c=2a=-8\text{ or }-4\end{align*}
Then $c-a$ is $$(-8)-(-4)=\boxed{-4}\text{ or }(-4)-(-2)=\boxed{-2}.$$ </answer>

